"""Explicitly authored problems, reading alarms and links; serialization only."""
import csv,json,pathlib
R=pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]
P=[]
def problem(n,title,occ,as_he,today,art,why,worth,refs,claims='',attribution='handwritten text'):
 P.append(dict(id=f'MP-SA14-{n}',title=title,occurrences=occ,as_he_states=as_he,plausible_today=today,state_of_art=art,why_open=why,worth=worth,refs=refs.split(';') if refs else [],claims=claims.split(';') if claims else [],attribution=attribution))
problem(401,'Weil and polarization input for determinant signs',['MPL_60_002-035_transcription.tex:216–243 (scan9)','MPL_60_002-035_transcription.tex:320,347–390,473–483 (scans11,13,17)'],'The weight and sign formulas are conditional on Weil/standard conjectures and an admitted selfduality.','For rational pure geometric Frobenius data with a specified alternating polarization, parity and positive normalized determinant follow; arbitrary pure sheaves need not be selfdual.','Deligne1974 Theorem1.6 and §2.6; Deligne1980 §§1.8,3.3 establish precise weights. They do not prove every standard conjecture or construct a polarization on every proposed motive.','The pages explicitly admit inputs; no reason for a later abandonment is documented.','Worth a short hypothesis audit, since separating purity, rationality and polarization prevents false counterexamples.','Deligne1974WeilI;Deligne1980WeilII;Jannsen1992Numerical','C-SA14-406;C-SA14-407;C-SA14-408;C-SA14-415')
problem(402,'Generic selfduality and extension',['MPL_60_002-035_transcription.tex:368–381 (scan13)'],'Selfduality is admitted, with a compressed generic-point remark whose ink is uncertain.','A given generic isomorphism of lisse sheaves on a normal integral scheme extends uniquely; its existence remains separate.','Stacks0BQM gives the required fundamental-group surjectivity.','The text does not explain whether the generic existence is assumed or claimed; no external source resolves the handwriting.','Worth resolving on the scans before attaching a universal motivic assertion.','Stacks0BQM','C-SA14-413')
problem(403,'Boundary type and weight bound',['MPL_60_002-035_transcription.tex:561–591 (scan21)','MPL_60_002-035_transcription.tex:617–636 (scan23)'],'With resolution and Weil conjectures, the boundary has the proposed lower type and indicated weights.','Precise cohomological weights can be stated for mixed lisse sheaves; a type bound requires a definition of the source type predicate.','Deligne1980 §§3.3–3.4 supplies weight inequalities. Wildeshaus Theorem6.1 supplies a distinct motivic boundary duality under resolution. Neither identifies the undefined type.','The source leaves its invariant unspecified here, rather than presenting a complete theorem.','Worth an archival definition search; a theorem search alone cannot recover the missing predicate.','Deligne1980WeilII;Wildeshaus2006Boundary','C-SA14-419;C-SA14-421')
problem(404,'One-unit boundary duality shift',['MPL_60_002-035_transcription.tex:679–698 (scan25)','MPL_60_036-070_transcription.tex:554–578 (scan61)'],'“à vérifier si c’est 1 ou −1” after the boundary duality formula.','For C∞=Cone(RΓ_c→RΓ), D C∞(E)=C∞(DE)[−1].','Independent dual-triangle proof and CALC-06; Stacks014D fixes the cone convention. Wildeshaus Theorem6.1 has the corresponding 2n−1 shift in his motivic convention.','The later page repeats derived language but does not visibly settle the sign; no other reason is documented.','Mathematically resolved here; scan review is still worthwhile to identify which sign was written.','Stacks014D;Wildeshaus2006Boundary','C-SA14-422;C-SA14-447')
problem(405,'Variation of Euler characteristic and determinant',['MPL_60_002-035_transcription.tex:736–766 (scan27)'],'A margin asks for variation with E and X or n.','Specify a geometric family or coefficient operation; base-field extension sends F to F^r, χ to χ and δ to δ^r, while exact sequences give determinant multiplicativity.','The elementary base-extension and exact-sequence cases are proved here; the curve local-constant product gives a more structured variation framework after fixing its data.','The margin contains a direction rather than hypotheses or a proposed conclusion.','Worth developing only after choosing a family; one short run can compare tame rank-one twists and conductor jumps.','Deligne1973Constants;Laumon1987Fourier','C-SA14-426')
problem(406,'Repairing the nonadditive generic extension',['MPL_60_002-035_transcription.tex:950–977 (scan35)','MPL_60_036-070_transcription.tex:20 (scan37)'],'The ordinary extension L* is reproached for nonadditivity; a global composition-factor construction is then rejected as insufficiently local and incompatible with étale extension.','Use the local decomposition group, normal unipotent subgroup and exact finite-group averaging on K₀; retain ordinary invariants as a different functor.','Deligne1973 §8 supplies the local quasi-unipotent/Weil–Deligne context. The handwritten averaging argument is independently checked.','The text itself gives the reason for rejecting the first recipe: locality and étale compatibility.','Worth a short categorical comparison; no claim that the first global recipe already has the needed functoriality should survive.','Deligne1973Constants','C-SA14-430;C-SA14-433;C-SA14-435')
problem(407,'Geometric local factors and absolute values',['MPL_60_036-070_transcription.tex:450–453 (scan55)'],'Problem(a): determine local α, μ, λ and characteristic polynomials, especially Frobenius absolute values, in geometric situations.','For a specified pure lisse sheaf on a curve over a finite field, use the monodromy filtration and local weights; α is computed from inertia and μ vanishes in the stated category.','Deligne1980 Theorem1.8.4 and Remark1.8.8 give the precise local purity theorem. Averaging alone does not compute the representation.','The question asks for geometric information not supplied by the preceding formal construction.','Worth a bounded worked degeneration example, starting with semistable rank two and finite tame inertia.','Deligne1980WeilII;Deligne1973Constants','C-SA14-443;C-SA14-440')
problem(408,'Computing the modified Euler characteristic',['MPL_60_036-070_transcription.tex:454 (scan55)','MPL_60_036-070_transcription.tex:538 (scan59)'],'Problem(b) asks for χ; scan59 points to Artin representations.','GOS plus the degree-weighted dimensions of the averaged stalks computes χ♮ on a smooth proper curve with a lisse open restriction.','Raynaud1965 §3 Theorem1, published1966, gives Grothendieck’s formula; Laumon1987(3.1.5.3) gives the conductor form.','The pages give a reference direction, not a full calculation.','Resolved in the stated curve setting; a useful next task is a convention-complete example with closed points of degree greater than one.','Raynaud1965Euler;Laumon1987Fourier','C-SA14-444')
problem(409,'Global determinant from local data, including its sign',['MPL_60_036-070_transcription.tex:455–456 (scan55)','MPL_60_036-070_transcription.tex:493–520 (scan57)','MPL_60_036-070_transcription.tex:538 (scan59)'],'Problem(c) asks to calculate δ; the following calculation reaches only a result modulo sign.','Fix additive character, differential and geometric Frobenius conventions, then express the global epsilon constant as a product of local constants.','Deligne1973 Theorem4.1 gives local constants; Laumon1987 Theorem3.2.1.1 proves the curve product formula for constructible complexes.','The manuscript explicitly says “modulo un signe”; the needed later local data are not specified here.','Worth a short translation of one example into Deligne–Laumon conventions; the general product theorem is established.','Deligne1970Constants;Deligne1973Constants;Laumon1987Fourier','C-SA14-446')
problem(410,'Questioned square of the normalized determinant',['MPL_60_036-070_transcription.tex:385–404 (scan53)','MPL_60_036-070_transcription.tex:493–520 (scan57)'],'A circled question asks whether δ♮²=q^χ♮; later formulas introduce local factors.','The square includes the local defect determinant product; after μ cancellation it simplifies in the stated category, but a square never determines its root sign.','Independent determinant derivation in proof_notes.md and CALC-09; the sign-sensitive replacement is the Laumon product theorem with extra data.','The question is visibly followed by a more elaborate calculation; one intermediate factor is inconsistent and needs scan review.','Worth a source-focused normalization audit; the elementary square identity itself is now checked.','Deligne1973Constants;Laumon1987Fourier','C-SA14-445')
problem(411,'Strict lower-niveau normalization',['MPL_60_036-070_transcription.tex:593–624 (scan63)','MPL_60_036-070_transcription.tex:659–679 (scan65)','MPL_60_036-070_transcription.tex:721–745 (scan67)','MPL_60_036-070_transcription.tex:775–791 (scan69)'],'Find λ of strictly smaller niveau making σ(λL)/(λL) a constant sign; revise the initial weight-range description using structured factors.','For a precisely specified σ-stable multiplicative subgroup H of allowed factors, ask whether the norm-one multiplier is a coboundary in H; fixed-point order parities give necessary tests.','Unrestricted solutions are elementary and explicitly computed. Targeted archive, arXiv, zbMATH and MathOverflow searches did not identify a theorem with the page’s undefined H or niveau.','The text rejects the trivial λ=1/L and changes its allowed-factor language; the exact restriction remains insufficiently defined in the supplied fragment.','High interest after archival clarification; one few-hour run can classify a finite divisor-support model, not solve an undefined general programme.','Deligne1973Constants','C-SA14-448;C-SA14-449;C-SA14-450;C-SA14-451')
problem(412,'One-dimensional factor diagram',['MPL_60_036-070_transcription.tex:775–791 (scan69)'],'A final n=1 factor diagram sketches how the proposed rational normalization should work.','After fixing the roots and their σ-orbits, check numerator/denominator multiplicities and fixed-point parities explicitly.','CALC-10 proves the rational involution algebra. No unique interpretation of all diagram indices was found in the opened literature.','The diagram is compressed and some factor indices remain ambiguous; no later outcome is documented.','Worth checking on the scans before using it as an algorithmic recipe.','Deligne1973Constants','C-SA14-451')
problem(413,'Haar uniqueness deferred to another chapter',['MPL_60_002-035_transcription.tex:258–283 (scan10)'],'The reused printed text invokes uniqueness of Haar measure and refers the proof elsewhere.','Every locally compact group has a nonzero left Haar measure, unique up to positive scalar.','Alfsen1963 proves existence and uniqueness; its covering ratios and approximate-additivity Proposition1.1 match the relevant mechanism, but its constructive proof is different from the draft’s ultrafilter step.','This is an editorial cross-reference in printed Bourbaki material, not a stated doubt of the handwritten author.','No new mathematical development is needed; locating the draft chapter counterpart would improve provenance.','Alfsen1963Haar;BourbakiIntegration1963','C-SA14-457','reused printed Bourbaki text')
problem(414,'Cut commutative-algebra antecedents and proofs',['MPL_60_002-035_transcription.tex:786–788,860–874 (scans28,32)','MPL_60_036-070_transcription.tex:5,27–37,353–359 (scans36,38,52)'],'Several printed exercises or proofs begin or end mid-statement, refer to missing exercises12/13 or omit internal page4–39.','Recover the missing antecedents before making a universal claim about the local flatness criterion, the cut derivation, the DVR subring or Proposition11.','Published Bourbaki metadata was checked, and Samuel1962Derivations identifies the cut derivation example externally. Other missing draft propositions and internal4–39 remain unidentified; draft numbers alone do not recover them.','The incompleteness is in this archival selection. It is not evidence that the published theorem remained open.','Worth a targeted source-recovery task; no speculative mathematical completion should be written into the transcription.','BourbakiAC57;Samuel1961UFD;Samuel1964Reflexive','C-SA14-480;C-SA14-481;C-SA14-482;C-SA14-483;C-SA14-492;C-SA14-498;C-SA14-502','reused printed Bourbaki fragments')
problem(415,'Distributions, jets and limits in the editorial report',['MPL_60_002-035_transcription.tex:404–410 (scan14)'],'The Bourbaki report asks why not inductive and projective limits while proposing to merge point distributions with jets.','This is a question about the scope and economy of an exposition; no single mathematical conjecture is specified.','Official Bourbaki publication metadata was checked for provenance. No theorem is inferred from an editorial question.','The report proposes removing or shortening material and reorganizing chapters.','Worth only an editorial-history source comparison, not a mathematical problem-solving run.','BourbakiIntegration1963','','reused printed Bourbaki editorial report')
problem(416,'Uncertain editorial assignments and possible homological-algebra publication',['MPL_60_002-035_transcription.tex:489–508 (scan18)'],'Question marks attach to Bastiani/probability assignments; homological algebra is mentioned as a possible publication.','These are assignments and a conditional publication intention, with no mathematical truth condition to modernize.','The supplied report is the checked witness; chapter metadata cannot prove that every plan was carried out.','The report itself marks the assignments or publication as uncertain.','No mathematical follow-up is warranted; preserve the archival distinction and avoid dating handwriting from the report.','BourbakiAC57','','reused printed Bourbaki editorial report')
(R/'problems.json').write_text(json.dumps(P,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n')
A=[]
def alarm(scan,package,line,reading,alternative,evidence,weight='high',confidence='high'):
 A.append(dict(cote=60,scan=scan,line=f'MPL_60_{package}_transcription.tex:{line}',reading=reading,alternative=alternative,evidence=evidence,weight=weight,confidence=confidence))
